-- title:
--   Lemma 4.3 — same type implies an edge path of length at most two
-- statement:
--   **Lemma 4.3 of Bux–Kassmann–Schulze.** If $x, y \in U$ have the same cone type, then there
--   is an edge path in $G[U]$ of length at most two connecting them.
--
--   Concretely: there is a point $z$ with an edge $x \to z$ and an edge $y \to z$. An edge
--   $a \to b$ of $G[U]$ means $a \in U$ and $b \in V^\Gamma[a]$, so the two edges exhibit the
--   path $x - z - y$.
--
--   **$z$ is not required to lie in $U$**, and the source says so explicitly: "We pick a point
--   of intersection (it may lie outside of $U$)." The edge relation constrains only the
--   *source* of an edge to lie in $U$, which is what makes the two-edge path available at all.
--
--   **The hypothesis is equality of the underlying sets**, $\Gamma(x)$ and $\Gamma(y)$ having
--   the same carrier, rather than equality of the cone data (axis and apex angle together).
--   That is the weaker assumption — distinct axis/angle pairs can describe the same set — so
--   this is slightly stronger than the source's "have the same type".
--
--   The proof is the source's: $V[x]$ and $V[y]$ intersect for the common type $V$, and any
--   point of the intersection receives an edge from each of $x$ and $y$. `QFS.connect_two_of_same_type`
--   records the same conclusion as connectivity in $G[U]$.
